course: dictionary
lesson: 4994
title: Words for believing without thinking
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Indoctrination is a noun, a word for a thing that happens. It means teaching someone to accept ideas or beliefs without questioning them or thinking for themselves. To find its meaning in a text, read the words around it, because they are its context.
explain: «"Re-education" means forced political indoctrination: people are made to repeat and accept the government's ideas, often in camps.» The clues are forced and made to repeat. If people are forced, they have no choice, so they cannot think for themselves.
explain: Indoctrination is one-sided. It means being trained to believe one side only. When you meet this word, ask: are people allowed to hear other views and to ask questions? If only one view is ever shared, the clues point to indoctrination.
fact: Indoctrination means teaching someone to accept ideas or beliefs without questioning them or thinking for themselves.
fact: «Political indoctrination» means training people to believe one side only in politics.
fact: «A form of indoctrination» means a kind of teaching that stops people from asking questions.
fact: Indoctrination is a noun with no plural, so you never write "indoctrinations".
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: Indoctrination means teaching someone to accept ideas without questioning them. What does "without questioning" mean? | Without eating any lunch | Without stopping to say hello | Without any help from teachers | *Without asking whether an idea is true
exercise: «"Re-education" means forced political indoctrination: people are made to repeat and accept the government's ideas.» Which word shows that people have no choice? | *forced | ideas | camps | political
exercise: Lily reads: "They were given only one side of the story, and never allowed to ask why." What is this a sign of? | *Indoctrination | A quiz with many answers | A fun day out | Good science lessons
test: Which of these is a form of indoctrination? | A class where pupils debate both sides of a question | *A class that is only ever told one side, and never asked what they think | A library with books on many views | Reading a map of the hills
test: Indoctrination is a noun with no plural. Which sentence uses it correctly? | *The teacher's indoctrination stopped the children from asking questions. | The indoctrinations were loud and long. | Many indoctrinations were in the book. | They indoctrinations the children every day.
exam: «Political indoctrination» is most like which idea? | Drawing a picture of the sea | Choosing your favourite colour | Sharing a snack with friends | *Teaching people to believe one side only and not to question it
